  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Determine the coordinates of the point of inflection of the graph of fx</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Let \(f(x) = {@fx@}\).</p>
<p>i. Determine the coordinates of the point of inflection of the graph of \(f(x)\).</p>
<p>\(\big( \) [[input:ans1]],&nbsp;[[input:ans2]] \(\big) \).</p>
<p>[[validation:ans1]]&nbsp;[[validation:ans2]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt1]]&nbsp;[[feedback:prt2]]<br></p>
<p>ii. Determine the intervals on which the graph of the function is concave up and concave down.</p><p>Concave up:&nbsp;[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p><p>Concave down:&nbsp;[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>In order to find points of inflection, we need to find the value of \(x\) for which the second derivative equals 0.</p><p>\(f(x) = {@fx@}\)</p><p>\(f'(x) = {@dfx@}\)<br></p><p>\(f''(x) = {@ddfx@} = 0\)<br></p><p>So, \(x = {@tainfx@}\) and \(f({@tainfx@}) = {@tainfy@}\). So, the coordinates of the point of inflection are \(({@tainfx@},{@tainfy@})\).</p><p>To determine the concave up and concave down intervals, we note that the concavity of a graph changes at a point of inflection. Checking the sign of the second derivative at a point before and after the point of inflection will inform us when the graph is concave up and concave down. For example, \(f''({@tainfx-1@}) = {@ev(ddfx,x=tainfx-1)@} &lt; 0\), which means that when \(x&lt;{@tainfx@}\) the graph is concave down. Similarly,&nbsp;\(f''({@tainfx+1@}) = {@ev(ddfx,x=tainfx+1)@} &gt; 0\), which means that when \(x&gt;{@tainfx@}\) the graph is concave up.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
